exports[`urlExtractor > extracts a blog article into heading-keyed evidence 1`] = `
{
  "assets": [],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "locator": {},
      "ref": "url:https://blog.example.com/vector-dbs#why-vector-databases-matter",
      "text": "Search used to mean matching keywords. Modern applications instead compare *meaning*, which requires turning text, images and audio into embeddings and finding the closest ones quickly.",
    },
    {
      "locator": {},
      "ref": "url:https://blog.example.com/vector-dbs#why-vector-databases-matter-2",
      "text": "A vector database stores those embeddings and answers nearest-neighbour queries. In our benchmarks, switching from brute-force search to an HNSW index cut p95 latency from 480 ms to 12 ms on a 10 million vector corpus.",
    },
    {
      "locator": {
        "selector": "#how-approximate-search-works",
      },
      "ref": "url:https://blog.example.com/vector-dbs#how-approximate-search-works",
      "text": "Exact search compares a query against every stored vector, which is too slow at scale. Approximate nearest neighbour (ANN) indexes trade a little recall for a large speed-up.",
    },
    {
      "locator": {
        "selector": "#how-approximate-search-works",
      },
      "ref": "url:https://blog.example.com/vector-dbs#how-approximate-search-works-2",
      "text": "- HNSW builds a layered proximity graph and typically reaches 95% recall.
- IVF partitions vectors into clusters and only scans the closest ones.
- Product quantization compresses vectors to roughly 1/16 of their original size.",
    },
    {
      "locator": {
        "selector": "#getting-started",
      },
      "ref": "url:https://blog.example.com/vector-dbs#getting-started",
      "text": "Most clients follow the same shape: create a collection, upsert vectors, then query.",
    },
    {
      "locator": {
        "selector": "#getting-started",
      },
      "ref": "url:https://blog.example.com/vector-dbs#getting-started-3",
      "text": "\`\`\`python
client = VectorClient(url="http://localhost:6333")
client.upsert("docs", vectors=embeddings)
hits = client.query("docs", vector=q, top_k=5)
\`\`\`",
    },
    {
      "locator": {
        "selector": "#getting-started",
      },
      "ref": "url:https://blog.example.com/vector-dbs#getting-started-4",
      "text": "Teams that adopted this pattern reported shipping semantic search 3x faster, and hosting costs fell by $1,200 per month.",
    },
    {
      "locator": {
        "selector": "#getting-started-2",
      },
      "ref": "url:https://blog.example.com/vector-dbs#getting-started-2",
      "text": "Yes, this heading is intentionally duplicated to exercise anchor uniqueness in the extractor tests.",
    },
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Search used to mean matching keywords. Modern applications instead compare meaning, which requires turning text, images and audio into embeddings and finding the closest ones quickly.

A vector database stores those embeddings and answers nearest-neighbour queries. In our benchmarks, switching from brute-force search to an HNSW index cut p95 latency from 480 ms to 12 ms on a 10 million vector corpus.",
    },
    {
      "heading": "How approximate search works",
      "text": "Exact search compares a query against every stored vector, which is too slow at scale. Approximate nearest neighbour (ANN) indexes trade a little recall for a large speed-up.

- HNSW builds a layered proximity graph and typically reaches 95% recall.
- IVF partitions vectors into clusters and only scans the closest ones.
- Product quantization compresses vectors to roughly 1/16 of their original size.",
    },
    {
      "heading": "Getting started",
      "text": "Most clients follow the same shape: create a collection, upsert vectors, then query.

\`\`\`python
client = VectorClient(url="http://localhost:6333")
client.upsert("docs", vectors=embeddings)
hits = client.query("docs", vector=q, top_k=5)
\`\`\`

Teams that adopted this pattern reported shipping semantic search 3x faster, and hosting costs fell by $1,200 per month.",
    },
    {
      "heading": "Getting started",
